# --- Adapter service ---
PORT=4100
HOST=0.0.0.0

# --- Optional TLS termination at the adapter ---
# Most deployments terminate TLS at an upstream ingress (load balancer,
# sidecar proxy, mesh) and leave these unset, so the adapter listens
# plain HTTP inside the private network. Set both to have Fastify
# itself terminate TLS — useful when there is no ingress in front of it.
# Both must be set together; partial config fails closed at startup.
# ADAPTER_TLS_CERT_PATH=/etc/cryptokorr/adapter.pem
# ADAPTER_TLS_KEY_PATH=/etc/cryptokorr/adapter.key

# --- Bidirectional bearer auth with Phoenix ---
# Two distinct secrets gate the two directions of the trust boundary.
# Each can be rotated independently and a leak in one direction does
# not authenticate the other. Generate each with `openssl rand -hex 32`
# and provision matching values to the Phoenix side.
#
#   * ADAPTER_DISPATCH_SECRET — bearer the adapter REQUIRES on inbound
#     POST /dispatch/* requests (Phoenix → adapter).
#   * ADAPTER_CALLBACK_SECRET — bearer the adapter SENDS on outbound
#     POST /internal/adapter/callback (adapter → Phoenix); Phoenix
#     validates it.
ADAPTER_DISPATCH_SECRET=dev-adapter-dispatch-secret
ADAPTER_CALLBACK_SECRET=dev-adapter-callback-secret

# --- Phoenix callback target ---
PHOENIX_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000

# --- Base Sepolia chain (public-alpha default) ---
# The public MVP path is testnet-only. Use Base mainnet only for
# operator-controlled experiments after the Sepolia runbooks pass.
BASE_RPC_URL=https://sepolia.base.org
BASE_CHAIN_ID=84532

# --- ERC-4337 v0.7 bundler ---
# Dedicated bundler JSON-RPC endpoint. In local dev can be set to
# BASE_RPC_URL if the provider supports eth_sendUserOperation on the
# same endpoint; otherwise point it at a Base Sepolia bundler
# (Pimlico, Alchemy AA, Stackup, etc.).
BUNDLER_RPC_URL=https://your-base-sepolia-bundler.example/rpc

# Address of the smart account this adapter controls (the UserOperation
# `sender`). Must be deployed and funded on the target chain. The
# adapter uses a single operator-managed account in v0.1.
SMART_ACCOUNT_ADDRESS=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# EntryPoint v0.7 canonical address — same on every EVM chain.
# Override only for test networks with a custom EntryPoint.
ENTRY_POINT_ADDRESS=0x0000000071727De22E5E9d8BAf0edAc6f37da032

# --- ZeroDev kernel permissions (formerly "PERMISSION_VALIDATOR_ADDRESS")
#     — REMOVED ---
# An earlier version of this template asked for a single
# `PERMISSION_VALIDATOR_ADDRESS` env var. That model was wrong:
# `@zerodev/permissions` does NOT have a single deployable Permission
# Validator contract. `toPermissionValidator()` returns a plugin
# whose `.address === zeroAddress`; permissions compose from CREATE2
# signer + policy modules (e.g. `ECDSA_SIGNER_CONTRACT`,
# `CALL_POLICY_CONTRACT_V0_0_5`) plus a 4-byte `permissionId`. Revoke
# is a call to `Kernel.uninstallValidation(...)` on the smart account
# itself, not on a separate validator contract. See
# `docs/zerodev-permissions-integration.md` for the full corrected
# model. The adapter's revoke path remains a sentinel UserOp until
# the SDK + per-account sudo signer + bundler integration described
# there lands.

# --- Smart account / delegation ---
# In production these come from a secrets manager.
# The adapter never holds end-user private keys directly;
# it uses delegation credentials scoped by the smart account. The key
# signs the UserOperation hash produced by the EntryPoint v0.7 schema.
DELEGATION_SIGNER_KEY=0x_dev_placeholder

# --- Kernel ROOT validator (sudo) signer for cryptographic revoke (#58) ---
# Optional. Both must be set together (or both unset to fall back to the
# sentinel revoke path). The runtime enforces:
#
#   * `OPERATOR_PRIVATE_KEY` is a 0x-prefixed 32-byte hex private key.
#   * `OPERATOR_ADDRESS` is the matching public EOA — derived address
#     must equal this value (paste-mismatch guard).
#   * The derived EOA must DIFFER from the EOA derived from
#     `DELEGATION_SIGNER_KEY` — refusing to conflate the kernel's
#     root validator with the runtime session signer is a defense-
#     in-depth invariant. A leak of the runtime session key MUST NOT
#     also disclose sudo authority over the smart account.
#   * Placeholders matching `placeholder` or `0x_` are refused.
#
# The kernel root validator binds to `OPERATOR_ADDRESS` at deploy time
# (see `scripts/provision-kernel.ts`); the same EOA is the only signer
# that can satisfy the kernel's `onlyEntryPointOrSelfOrRoot` guard on
# `Kernel.uninstallValidation(...)`. When unset the adapter refuses to
# honor any dispatch carrying a `permission` block and emits
# `revoke_failed` so Phoenix can re-issue or escalate.
#
# Provision via secrets manager in production. Never commit a real key.
# OPERATOR_PRIVATE_KEY=0x_dev_placeholder
# OPERATOR_ADDRESS=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# --- USDC on Base Sepolia ---
USDC_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=0x036cbd53842c5426634e7929541ec2318f3dcf7e

# --- Contract version ---
CONTRACT_VERSION=1
